  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 101 PM CST Sun Nov 8 2026\n\nOverall a mostly dry and seasonable to mild pattern will be in\nplace through the week.\n\nA dry cold front will move southeast through the forecast area\ntonight, bringing little more than a wind shift and some mid\nlevel clouds. Lows are expected to range from the mid 30s to\nlower 40s. Highs Monday, behind the cold front will be mainly \nin the 50s. \n\nAnother clipper system and it's associated surface low will \nmove through the area Tuesday. The low predictable nature of \nthis system is reflected in that the NBM only now beginning to \nindicate some low precipitation chances due to the large\nenvelope of ensemble solutions. Wouldn't be surprised to see \nchances rise as model agreement increases. Any precipitation \namounts are expected to be light. Currently, the NBM is \ndepicting a 20-30 percent probability for at least a tenth of \nan inch of liquid in the 24 hour period ending late Tuesday \nevening (most of which would fall during the day Tuesday), in \nabout a 50-70 mile wide swath from northwest to southeast ND. \nDepending on the timing of the wave, we could see some snow or a\nmix of rain and snow as the system enters western ND Tuesday \nmorning. Any snow amounts would be negligible and by mid to late\nmorning precip is forecast to be all rain.\n\nThis wave does help to deepen an upper level trough over the \neastern U.S. and will allow for a chunk of colder Canadian air \nto drop south through Minnesota and the eastern two thirds of \nNorth Dakota. This will bring a significant cooldown, \nespecially to eastern portions of central ND for Wednesday \nthrough Friday. There is about a 20 degree difference in high \ntemperatures from far eastern to far western ND each day. \nHowever, the mid 30s forecast for the James River Valley on \nThursday are still within 4-8 degrees of the normal high at \nJamestown for this time of year. Definitely seasonable, but on \nthe cool side of normal. That goes to show how mild we have \nbeen, and how mild it will continue to be over western ND. There\nis also some disagreement in the placement and extent of the \ncold air mid to late week. Current NBM ensemble spreads are on \nthe order of 15 to 20 degrees for the Wednesday through Friday \ntimeframe for all major sites. We'll see how they eventually \nend up, but a large range in temperatures (especially daytime \nhighs) from west to east looks to be in the cards.\n\nBy the weekend, upper level ridging is situated over the area,\nkeeping it dry and mild with widespread 50s and 60s expected \nacross the forecast area.",
